  "name": "Nivkh music (bear-festival tree drum, tyngryng and jaw harp)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Music of the Nivkh, a fishing people of the lower Amur and northern Sakhalin. At the bear festival women beat a suspended dry log, the musical tree, with sticks, represented here by a generic auxiliary-percussion card; the one-string tyngryng fiddle and the jaw harp, represented by the khomus card, are played solo and with song. The repertoire is preserved in the Pushkin House phonogram archive and Sakhalin field recordings.",
  "recipe": "classic early-1940s nivkh music, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. credited auxiliary percussion, fish skin hollow rasping low tension drone under melody tyngryng, khomus. field recording. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, portable field-recorder pre. tape 15ips.",
